<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chamber of rhetoric</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as rederijkerskamer</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chambers of rhetoric were dramatic societies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a> whose members, the rederijkers, took their name from the French rhétoricien and in the 15th and 16th centuries cultivated drama and lyric poetry. The first were founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> around the 15th century; they later flowered in Holland, where most cities sponsored one or more chambers that competed in prize contests, and De Egelantier in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> counted Coster, Bredero, Hooft and Roemer Visscher among its members. Closely tied to civic leaders, the chambers performed at civic festivities and, like <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a>, attended members' funerals and collected for the sick and poor. The play Elckerlijc (Everyman) came from them.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chamber-of-rhetoric.jpg" alt="Chamber of rhetoric" width="330" height="395" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jan Steen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Rhetoricians,_circa_1655,_by_Jan_Steen_(1625-1679)_-_IMG_7324.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
